> **JohnFP:** Hey Pablo, I ran a few tests tonight on SM and Alum but it started to storm and I didn't want to run my water cooled laser during storm, my water stops when electricity goes off.Anyway, my tests were with SM and ALum. I tried 1000ml water and 25g SM and 5g Alum. It took the blue out much too quick. But I got an OK hologram but green, save wavlength I was using (514), maybe a tadd shorter. It seem very narrow band as it was visible in very nicely in laser but didn't seem too bring in white light.I added another 1000ml water to the fixer. This time the reducer worked as expected maybe still just a tad strong. The hologram came out the same color, a little less bright and a little more milky.My next step was to add another 250ml water for the reducer. And to get the Alum back to it's original concentration, i added 6.25g.My thinking is, get the reducer to work like it should, then add or subtract hardener as needed for softer or harder hardening. This should be real close.But I did not get a bright hologram yet, so we have to wait until I do to prove it works.Tomorrow I hope to continue tests.

John you might have to backward engineer this. This is a acid base fixer. Read page 153 sections 4.4:3 ,4.4.4, 4.4.5 in Siver-Halide Recording Materials by H.I. Bjelkhagen, in these sections it show what each element does and why its added.

40-45% water; 

42% ammonium thiosulfate; 

5-10% sodium acetate; 

1-5% boric acid, 

1-5% ammonium sulfite, 

1-5% acetic acid; 

1% sodium sulfite
